{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-b48d883ef00f037e", "type": "tool_result", "content": "4069 interactvid_full.txt\nTsinghua University\nAugust 4, 2026\n\nInteracVid: Building a Real Interactive Audio-Visual\nResponse Dataset from Live-Chat Videos\nChi Zhang\n\nHaoyang Shi\n\nYueyi Liu\n\nZhaokun Yan\n\nYishu Yin\n\nYuhang Wu\n\nMiao Liu†\n\nCollege of AI, Tsinghua University\nimzc.2004@gmail.com miaoliu@mail.tsinghua.edu.cn\n\narXiv:2608.01157v1 [cs.CV] 2 Aug 2026\n\nProject Page\n\nDataset\n\nCode\n\nAbstract. Large language models have made text the default medium for human–AI interaction,\nbut text alone cannot express the full range of responses required by multimodal assistants,\navatars, and embodied agents. While recent audio-video generative models can synthesize\nhigh-fidelity synchronized content, existing supervision is largely descriptive: models are\ntrained to render captions rather than to produce audio-visual responses caused by external\nuser interactions. We introduce InteracVid, the first open-source large-scale dataset that\naddresses this missing supervision, so that every sample couples a preceding audio-visual\ncontext and an external stimulus with the real interactive response that follows. We design a\nmetadata-aware pipeline that extracts interactive clips from long, noisy livestreams, yielding\nover 454K context-query-response triplets from more than 59K livestream videos and spanning\nconversation-centered, object-centric, procedural, embodied, and screen-based scenarios. A\nten-rater human study confirms that the extracted interactions are causal, natural, and\ntemporally complete for both genuine and reconstructed queries. On a held-out benchmark of\n100 genuine live-chat queries, fine-tuning on InteracVid improves both interaction planning\nand audio-video response generation, and an independent human evaluation reproduces the\nsystem ranking and the conclusions obtained with our automatic judge. These results highlight\ninteraction-structured data as a critical foundation for interactive multimodal generation.\n\n1 Introduction\nDriven by the success of large language models [3, 8, 14, 27, 46], text has become the most prevailing\nmedia for human–AI interaction. However, text-based interactions remain constrained by the\nresponse modality and struggle with expressiveness and multimodal grounding. As AI systems\nmove from text chatbots toward multimodal assistants, avatars, and embodied agents, interaction\nshould no longer be limited to generating the next utterance. A model should be able to generate an\naudio-visual response that is appropriate to the user’s query and grounded in the visual context.\nRecent audio-video generative models have substantially improved the fidelity and synchronization\nof multimodal synthesis. Existing systems can generate talking faces [22, 23, 48, 55, 61, 71, 74],\nspeech-driven video generation [30, 31, 33, 62, 65, 75], conversational avatars [12, 18, 35, 39, 58, 61, 67],\nand text-conditioned audio-video clips [25, 32, 40, 41, 43–45, 54]. However, most of these systems\nremain primarily descriptive: they render multimodal content specified by input captions rather\n1\n\n\fTsinghua University\n\nOur Setting: User Query-Conditioned Audio-Video Generation\nInput\n\nOutput\n\n|\n\nTotal Pairs\n\nReal Pairs\n\nSynthetic Pairs\n\nUnique Videos\n\n1080p ratio\n\n454K+\n\n39K+\n\n414K+\n\n59K+\n\n66%+\n\n2\n\nVideo Generation\nAudiovisual Context\n\nDiverse Video\nDomains\n\nDataset Grid (Examples)\n…\n\nAudio Generation\nUser Query\n\nKeep it\n\nWhat cuisine do you\nmiss most that you\ncan’t get in Vietnam?\n\nwhere's the\nremaining body\n\nwhat is their lifespan?\n\nI remember when\nthat happened to\nyou\n\nHow is your body pain\nafter yesterday’s live\nstream Christine?\n\nHow much time does it\ntake from start to finish\ncomplete a model?\n\nhow do you multitask\nbetween … and building\nat the same time?\n\nFor 40k?\n\nis it cheating if i\nsteam them?\n\nQuestion: Does that\nCanadian wine have\nbilingual labeling?\n\nAudio Transcript\nY’all, I'mma keep it because I do like this.\n\nTraditional Audio-Video Generation Setting\nInput\n\nOutput\nVideo Generation\n\nCaption (User-specified)\ndo you have a pattern The modkit nightmare\nfor the cardigan in the might as well be the\nback?\nnew subtitle\n\nA woman in a room with abstract\nwall art, wearing a beige blazer and\nwhite T-shirt, gestures and speaks.\nAudio Transcript (User-specified)\n\nY’all, I'mma keep it because I do\nlike this.\n\nAudio Generation\n\nDiverse real-world\nuser queries\n\nWide range of video\ndomains and styles\n\nRich audio and\ntranscriptions\n\nTemporally & semantically\naligned pairs\n\nFigure 1. Overview of InteracVid. We target an important yet underexplored problem of interactive\naudio-video generation, providing a large-scale, high-quality dataset with diverse video domains. Each sample\nkeeps the full interaction structure: a preceding audio-visual context, an external live-chat query, and the\nreal audio-visual response that follows.\n\nthan producing responses caused by an external interaction. In real interactive scenarios, a user\nquery does not merely describe the desired output. It acts as a stimulus that may lead to a spoken\nanswer, a gesture, a facial reaction, an object manipulation, a screen-based action, or a visually\ngrounded demonstration.\nTo train an audio-video model that can respond interactively, we need examples that preserve the\nfull interaction structure: the interaction context, the user stimulus, and the resulting audio-visual\nresponse. Existing audio-video datasets [10, 20, 73] are usually prepared for descriptive generation\ntraining, and do not identify the reactive video segments or provide a user-side query. Interactive\ntalking-head and avatar datasets [9, 73] move closer to response generation, but they are mostly\nrestricted to human speaking behavior. Importantly, current datasets do not provide sufficient\nsupervision for learning query-triggered, context-grounded, interactive audio-visual responses.\nLivestream videos provide a natural source for this missing supervision. In livestreams, viewers\nengage in live-chats, sending time-synchronized comments, questions, and requests, while streamers\nreact in speech and action within the continuous context. These live-chats act as external interaction\ntriggers, and the following streamer behavior provides naturally grounded audio-visual responses.\nHowever, converting livestreams into training data is non-trivial: livestreams are long, noisy, sparsely\ninteractive, and only a small fraction of comments are actually answered. Moreover, live-chat\nShell cwd was reset to /inspire/qb-ilm/project/video-generation/chenxie-25019/hyr/iHateTheWorld555.github.io", "is_error": false}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "084bfb49-665f-4b6b-8d17-78ca4000b371", "uuid": "90fe5c8b-7f1e-4e10-a99e-87871ee8b060", "timestamp": "2026-08-04T17:02:09.130Z", "tool_use_result": {"stdout": "4069 interactvid_full.txt\nTsinghua University\nAugust 4, 2026\n\nInteracVid: Building a Real Interactive Audio-Visual\nResponse Dataset from Live-Chat Videos\nChi Zhang\n\nHaoyang Shi\n\nYueyi Liu\n\nZhaokun Yan\n\nYishu Yin\n\nYuhang Wu\n\nMiao Liu†\n\nCollege of AI, Tsinghua University\nimzc.2004@gmail.com miaoliu@mail.tsinghua.edu.cn\n\narXiv:2608.01157v1 [cs.CV] 2 Aug 2026\n\nProject Page\n\nDataset\n\nCode\n\nAbstract. Large language models have made text the default medium for human–AI interaction,\nbut text alone cannot express the full range of responses required by multimodal assistants,\navatars, and embodied agents. While recent audio-video generative models can synthesize\nhigh-fidelity synchronized content, existing supervision is largely descriptive: models are\ntrained to render captions rather than to produce audio-visual responses caused by external\nuser interactions. We introduce InteracVid, the first open-source large-scale dataset that\naddresses this missing supervision, so that every sample couples a preceding audio-visual\ncontext and an external stimulus with the real interactive response that follows. We design a\nmetadata-aware pipeline that extracts interactive clips from long, noisy livestreams, yielding\nover 454K context-query-response triplets from more than 59K livestream videos and spanning\nconversation-centered, object-centric, procedural, embodied, and screen-based scenarios. A\nten-rater human study confirms that the extracted interactions are causal, natural, and\ntemporally complete for both genuine and reconstructed queries. On a held-out benchmark of\n100 genuine live-chat queries, fine-tuning on InteracVid improves both interaction planning\nand audio-video response generation, and an independent human evaluation reproduces the\nsystem ranking and the conclusions obtained with our automatic judge. These results highlight\ninteraction-structured data as a critical foundation for interactive multimodal generation.\n\n1 Introduction\nDriven by the success of large language models [3, 8, 14, 27, 46], text has become the most prevailing\nmedia for human–AI interaction. However, text-based interactions remain constrained by the\nresponse modality and struggle with expressiveness and multimodal grounding. As AI systems\nmove from text chatbots toward multimodal assistants, avatars, and embodied agents, interaction\nshould no longer be limited to generating the next utterance. A model should be able to generate an\naudio-visual response that is appropriate to the user’s query and grounded in the visual context.\nRecent audio-video generative models have substantially improved the fidelity and synchronization\nof multimodal synthesis. Existing systems can generate talking faces [22, 23, 48, 55, 61, 71, 74],\nspeech-driven video generation [30, 31, 33, 62, 65, 75], conversational avatars [12, 18, 35, 39, 58, 61, 67],\nand text-conditioned audio-video clips [25, 32, 40, 41, 43–45, 54]. However, most of these systems\nremain primarily descriptive: they render multimodal content specified by input captions rather\n1\n\n\fTsinghua University\n\nOur Setting: User Query-Conditioned Audio-Video Generation\nInput\n\nOutput\n\n|\n\nTotal Pairs\n\nReal Pairs\n\nSynthetic Pairs\n\nUnique Videos\n\n1080p ratio\n\n454K+\n\n39K+\n\n414K+\n\n59K+\n\n66%+\n\n2\n\nVideo Generation\nAudiovisual Context\n\nDiverse Video\nDomains\n\nDataset Grid (Examples)\n…\n\nAudio Generation\nUser Query\n\nKeep it\n\nWhat cuisine do you\nmiss most that you\ncan’t get in Vietnam?\n\nwhere's the\nremaining body\n\nwhat is their lifespan?\n\nI remember when\nthat happened to\nyou\n\nHow is your body pain\nafter yesterday’s live\nstream Christine?\n\nHow much time does it\ntake from start to finish\ncomplete a model?\n\nhow do you multitask\nbetween … and building\nat the same time?\n\nFor 40k?\n\nis it cheating if i\nsteam them?\n\nQuestion: Does that\nCanadian wine have\nbilingual labeling?\n\nAudio Transcript\nY’all, I'mma keep it because I do like this.\n\nTraditional Audio-Video Generation Setting\nInput\n\nOutput\nVideo Generation\n\nCaption (User-specified)\ndo you have a pattern The modkit nightmare\nfor the cardigan in the might as well be the\nback?\nnew subtitle\n\nA woman in a room with abstract\nwall art, wearing a beige blazer and\nwhite T-shirt, gestures and speaks.\nAudio Transcript (User-specified)\n\nY’all, I'mma keep it because I do\nlike this.\n\nAudio Generation\n\nDiverse real-world\nuser queries\n\nWide range of video\ndomains and styles\n\nRich audio and\ntranscriptions\n\nTemporally & semantically\naligned pairs\n\nFigure 1. Overview of InteracVid. We target an important yet underexplored problem of interactive\naudio-video generation, providing a large-scale, high-quality dataset with diverse video domains. Each sample\nkeeps the full interaction structure: a preceding audio-visual context, an external live-chat query, and the\nreal audio-visual response that follows.\n\nthan producing responses caused by an external interaction. In real interactive scenarios, a user\nquery does not merely describe the desired output. It acts as a stimulus that may lead to a spoken\nanswer, a gesture, a facial reaction, an object manipulation, a screen-based action, or a visually\ngrounded demonstration.\nTo train an audio-video model that can respond interactively, we need examples that preserve the\nfull interaction structure: the interaction context, the user stimulus, and the resulting audio-visual\nresponse. Existing audio-video datasets [10, 20, 73] are usually prepared for descriptive generation\ntraining, and do not identify the reactive video segments or provide a user-side query. Interactive\ntalking-head and avatar datasets [9, 73] move closer to response generation, but they are mostly\nrestricted to human speaking behavior. Importantly, current datasets do not provide sufficient\nsupervision for learning query-triggered, context-grounded, interactive audio-visual responses.\nLivestream videos provide a natural source for this missing supervision. In livestreams, viewers\nengage in live-chats, sending time-synchronized comments, questions, and requests, while streamers\nreact in speech and action within the continuous context. These live-chats act as external interaction\ntriggers, and the following streamer behavior provides naturally grounded audio-visual responses.\nHowever, converting livestreams into training data is non-trivial: livestreams are long, noisy, sparsely\ninteractive, and only a small fraction of comments are actually answered. Moreover, live-chat", "stderr": "\nShell cwd was reset to /inspire/qb-ilm/project/video-generation/chenxie-25019/hyr/iHateTheWorld555.github.io", "interrupted": false, "isImage": false, "noOutputExpected": false}}
